-- Q1: hard / L3 — Two-class method for participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glenwood Industries has net income of $1,200,000 and 400,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding. Glenwood also has 50,000 shares of participating preferred stock entitled to receive dividends on a per-share basis equal to common shareholders after receiving a $2 per-share preference. Declared dividends total $500,000. Under ASC 260, how does the participating preferred stock affect basic EPS computation?', '["Only the $2 per-share preferred preference is deducted from the numerator", "The participating preferred is ignored because it only affects diluted EPS", "Undistributed earnings must be allocated between common and participating preferred using the two-class method", "The full $500,000 in dividends is deducted regardless of participation rights"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, when an entity has participating securities, it must use the two-class method to compute basic EPS. This method allocates undistributed earnings between common stock and participating securities based on their respective participation rights. Choice A is incorrect because deducting only the preference ignores the participation feature, which entitles preferred holders to share in remaining earnings alongside common shareholders.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Anti-dilution sequencing order
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Corp. reports net income of $2,000,000 with 500,000 weighted-average common shares. It has three potentially dilutive securities: (1) 10,000 stock options with 5,000 incremental shares via the treasury stock method, (2) $600,000 of 5% convertible bonds convertible into 25,000 shares (tax rate 25%), and (3) 8,000 shares of $50 par, 7% convertible preferred, each converting into 3 common shares. Evaluate the per-share incremental effects and recommend the correct inclusion sequence.', '["Options, preferred stock, convertible bonds", "Options, convertible bonds, convertible preferred stock", "Convertible bonds, options, convertible preferred stock", "Convertible preferred stock, convertible bonds, options"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, potentially dilutive securities are ranked from most to least dilutive based on their per-share incremental impact. Options add zero to the numerator ($0.00 per share), bonds add $22,500 after tax for 25,000 shares ($0.90 per share), and preferred adds $28,000 for 24,000 shares ($1.17 per share). The correct sequence is most dilutive first: options, bonds, preferred. Choice A incorrectly places preferred before bonds, reversing the proper dilution ranking.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: easy / L2 — Reverse stock split weighted-average calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Birchfield Corp. had 300,000 shares outstanding all year and executed a 1-for-3 reverse stock split on June 1. What is the weighted-average number of shares for the year?', '["300,000", "200,000", "183,333", "100,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits and reverse stock splits are applied retroactively as if they occurred at the beginning of the earliest period presented. Birchfield''s 300,000 shares become 100,000 shares (300,000 / 3) for the entire year, regardless of the June 1 effective date. Choice C (183,333) incorrectly prorates the reverse split, treating it as a mid-year event rather than applying it retroactively.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Simple vs. complex capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what distinguishes a complex capital structure from a simple capital structure?', '["A complex capital structure includes potentially dilutive securities such as options, warrants, or convertible instruments", "A complex capital structure has more than one class of common stock outstanding", "A complex capital structure requires at least $10 million in total equity", "A complex capital structure exists when the entity has both debt and equity financing"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-05-3, an entity has a complex capital structure when it has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute basic EPS, such as stock options, warrants, convertible debt, or convertible preferred stock. Choice B is incorrect because multiple classes of common stock alone do not create a complex structure unless those classes include conversion features or other dilutive potential.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Discontinued operations separate EPS presentation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stratford Holdings reports income from continuing operations of $750,000 and a $200,000 after-tax loss from discontinued operations (net income $550,000). Stratford has 100,000 weighted-average shares and 5,000 dilutive stock options producing 2,000 incremental shares. What is the most likely effect of the discontinued operations on EPS presentation?', '["Only a single diluted EPS figure of $5.39 is reported, combining continuing and discontinued operations", "Separate per-share amounts are reported for continuing operations and discontinued operations, with dilutive shares included in both computations", "Discontinued operations EPS is reported using basic shares only, while continuing operations uses diluted shares", "The discontinued operations loss eliminates the requirement to present diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, entities must present per-share amounts for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. Discontinued operations per-share amounts may be presented on the face or in the notes. The same denominator (including dilutive shares) is used for all EPS computations. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 requires separate presentation of continuing operations EPS, not a single combined figure.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Year-to-date EPS subtraction flaw
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinecrest Inc. reports year-to-date diluted EPS of $6.80 through Q3 and full-year diluted EPS of $8.50. An analyst calculates Q4 standalone diluted EPS as $1.70 by subtracting the Q3 year-to-date figure from the full-year figure. What is the most likely flaw in this approach?', '["The analyst used basic EPS instead of diluted EPS for the subtraction", "The analyst failed to account for stock splits that occurred in Q4", "Year-to-date EPS figures cannot be subtracted because the weighted-average share denominators may differ across periods", "Q4 EPS must equal one-quarter of the full-year EPS amount"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, EPS is computed independently for each period using that period''s weighted-average shares. Because the denominator can change between quarters (due to share issuances, repurchases, or varying dilutive effects), subtracting year-to-date EPS figures does not produce a valid quarterly result. Choice D is incorrect because quarterly EPS is not simply one-fourth of annual EPS, as income and share counts vary across periods.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Warrants treasury stock method incremental shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hartford Dynamics has 150,000 weighted-average common shares and net income of $450,000. The company has 8,000 warrants outstanding with an exercise price of $30. The average market price during the year was $50. Using the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are added for diluted EPS?', '["3,200", "4,800", "8,000", "2,400"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes warrants are exercised at the beginning of the period. The 8,000 warrants would generate $240,000 in proceeds (8,000 x $30), which could repurchase 4,800 shares at the $50 average market price. The incremental shares are 8,000 issued minus 4,800 repurchased, equaling 3,200. Choice B (4,800) incorrectly represents the shares repurchased rather than the net incremental shares.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Definition of diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what does diluted earnings per share represent?', '["Net income divided by the number of shares actually outstanding at year-end", "Operating income divided by weighted-average common shares", "Net income available to common shareholders divided by total authorized shares", "Earnings per share assuming all potentially dilutive securities are exercised or converted"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, diluted EPS reflects the potential dilution that would occur if all outstanding options, warrants, and convertible securities were exercised or converted into common stock. It represents the worst-case dilution scenario for existing shareholders. Choice A describes shares outstanding at a point in time rather than the weighted-average concept with dilutive adjustments that ASC 260 requires.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Who must present EPS (public entities)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement under ASC 260?', '["All entities that prepare financial statements under U.S. GAAP", "Entities with publicly held common stock or potential common stock", "Only SEC registrants with annual revenues exceeding $100 million", "All entities with more than one class of stock outstanding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required for entities that have issued common stock or potential common stock that trades in a public market, including stock exchanges and over-the-counter markets. Nonpublic entities are not required to present EPS. Choice A is incorrect because private companies following U.S. GAAP are specifically exempted from the EPS presentation requirements.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Share repurchase mid-year weighted-average reduction
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On January 1, Colton Enterprises has 240,000 shares of common stock outstanding. On September 1, Colton repurchases 36,000 shares as treasury stock. No other share transactions occurred during the year. What is the weighted-average number of shares for the year?', '["204,000", "240,000", "228,000", "222,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, weighted-average shares reflect the time period each share count was outstanding. From January 1 to August 31 (8 months), 240,000 shares were outstanding (240,000 x 8/12 = 160,000). From September 1 to December 31 (4 months), 204,000 shares remained (204,000 x 4/12 = 68,000). The weighted average is 228,000. Choice A (204,000) incorrectly uses only the ending share count without weighting.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Dual presentation requirement (basic + diluted)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what must an entity with a complex capital structure present on the face of its income statement?', '["Both basic and diluted earnings per share for each period presented", "Only diluted earnings per share, since it is more conservative", "Basic earnings per share only, with diluted EPS disclosed in the notes", "Either basic or diluted earnings per share, at management''s discretion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, an entity with a complex capital structure must present both basic and diluted EPS with equal prominence on the face of the income statement for income from continuing operations and net income. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 mandates dual presentation; reporting only diluted EPS would omit the basic EPS figure that investors use to assess actual per-share results.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Convertible bond premium and if-converted numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornberry Corp. has $1,000,000 face value of 8% convertible bonds issued at 104 (premium of $40,000). Annual premium amortization is $4,000. The tax rate is 25%. When testing these bonds for dilution using the if-converted method, what after-tax amount is added to the numerator?', '["$60,000, based on the $80,000 coupon only", "$63,000, based on the coupon plus premium amortization", "$30,000, representing the premium amortization adjusted for taxes", "$57,000, based on interest expense of $76,000 adjusted for taxes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-16, the if-converted method adds back the after-tax interest expense that would not have been recognized had conversion occurred. Interest expense includes the stated coupon less premium amortization ($80,000 - $4,000 = $76,000), adjusted for tax at 25%, producing $57,000. Choice A is incorrect because it uses the cash coupon rather than the GAAP interest expense, which accounts for premium amortization under ASC 835.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Definition of weighted-average shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What does the term weighted-average common shares outstanding represent in EPS calculations?', '["The total number of authorized shares available for issuance during the period", "The number of shares outstanding at the end of the reporting period", "The number of common shares outstanding weighted by the portion of the period they were outstanding", "The average of the beginning and ending share counts divided by two"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, weighted-average common shares outstanding reflects the number of shares times the fraction of the period during which they were outstanding. Shares issued mid-period are weighted from the date of issuance. Choice D is incorrect because a simple average of beginning and ending balances does not account for the specific dates of share transactions during the period.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Stock compensation awards treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Clearfield Technologies granted 12,000 restricted stock units (RSUs) to employees on January 1, 2025. The average unamortized compensation cost is $180,000, and the average market price during the year is $45 per share. Under the treasury stock method for diluted EPS, how many incremental shares result from the RSUs?', '["12,000", "8,000", "4,000", "6,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28A, the treasury stock method for stock compensation uses unamortized compensation cost as assumed proceeds. The $180,000 in unrecognized cost could hypothetically repurchase 4,000 shares at the $45 average price. The incremental dilutive shares are 12,000 granted minus 4,000 repurchased, yielding 8,000. Choice A (12,000) incorrectly assumes all RSUs are incremental without considering the treasury stock offset.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Continuing operations loss as control number
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Summit Alliance reports a loss from continuing operations of $300,000 and a $500,000 gain from discontinued operations, producing net income of $200,000. The company has 100,000 common shares and 5,000 dilutive stock options producing 2,000 incremental shares. What is the effect of these options on diluted EPS?', '["The options are excluded from diluted EPS because including them would be antidilutive given the loss from continuing operations", "The options are included because net income is positive, adding 2,000 shares to the denominator", "The options reduce diluted EPS from $2.00 to $1.96 based on net income", "The options affect only the discontinued operations per-share amount, not continuing operations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, the control number for determining whether securities are dilutive is income from continuing operations, not net income. Since Summit has a loss from continuing operations, including the options would reduce the loss per share (making it less negative), which is antidilutive. Therefore, the options are excluded from all diluted EPS computations. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 uses continuing operations income as the control number, regardless of net income.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Retroactive restatement of prior-period EPS for stock dividend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northfield Corp. reported basic EPS of $4.00 for 2024 based on net income of $800,000 and 200,000 weighted-average shares. On March 1, 2025, Northfield declares a 20% stock dividend. When presenting comparative 2025 and 2024 financial statements, the restated 2024 EPS is:', '["$4.00, because prior-period EPS is not restated for subsequent stock dividends", "$3.20, reflecting a 20% reduction in the numerator", "$4.80, because the stock dividend increases the prior-period EPS", "$3.33, reflecting a restated denominator of 240,000 shares"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends and stock splits are applied retroactively to all prior periods presented. Northfield''s 2024 weighted-average shares are restated from 200,000 to 240,000 (200,000 x 1.20), reducing EPS from $4.00 to $3.33 ($800,000 / 240,000). The numerator is unchanged. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 requires retroactive adjustment to ensure comparability across periods.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Contingently issuable shares conditions not met
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redstone Manufacturing has 200,000 common shares outstanding and has agreed to issue 25,000 additional shares to former owners of an acquired business if cumulative revenues exceed $50 million by December 31, 2025. As of December 31, 2025, cumulative revenues total $42 million. How are the 25,000 contingent shares treated for basic and diluted EPS?', '["Included in both basic and diluted EPS because the shares are legally committed", "Included in both basic EPS and diluted EPS based on the acquisition agreement", "Excluded from basic EPS and excluded from diluted EPS because the revenue condition is not met at period end", "Excluded from basic EPS but included in diluted EPS regardless of whether conditions are met"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS only if all conditions are satisfied by the end of the reporting period. For diluted EPS under ASC 260-10-45-51, contingent shares are included only if the conditions would be met based on current-period results. Since the $50 million threshold was not reached, the shares are excluded from both. Choice D is incorrect because diluted EPS does not automatically include all contingent shares; the conditions must be assessable as satisfied.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Definition of antidilutive securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what are antidilutive securities?', '["Securities that decrease the denominator when included in EPS calculations", "Securities whose inclusion would increase EPS or decrease loss per share", "Securities that have an exercise price above par value", "Securities that have been retired during the reporting period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, antidilutive securities are those whose assumed exercise or conversion would increase earnings per share or decrease loss per share from continuing operations. Antidilutive securities are excluded from the diluted EPS calculation. Choice A is incorrect because antidilutive securities typically affect the numerator and denominator in a way that produces a higher EPS, not necessarily by decreasing the denominator alone.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Rights issue bonus element adjustment factor
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Elliston Corp. has 500,000 shares outstanding and issues rights to existing shareholders to purchase one new share for every five held at $15, when the market price is $25. All rights are exercised on April 1. Under ASC 260, the theoretical ex-rights fair value is used to compute an adjustment factor. The adjustment factor is closest to:', '["1.071", "1.200", "1.000", "0.933"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-15, a rights issue at below market price contains a bonus element requiring an adjustment factor. The theoretical ex-rights price is ($25 x 5 + $15 x 1) / 6 = $23.33. The adjustment factor equals the pre-rights market price divided by the ex-rights price: $25 / $23.33 = 1.071. Choice B (1.200) incorrectly uses the ratio of total post-exercise to pre-exercise shares without accounting for the theoretical price adjustment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Written put options reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Industries has 200,000 common shares outstanding, net income of $600,000, and has written put options obligating it to repurchase 10,000 shares at $50 per share. The average market price during the year is $40. What is the effect of these written put options on diluted EPS?', '["No effect because put options are always antidilutive", "The denominator is reduced by 10,000 shares, increasing EPS", "The numerator is reduced by the $500,000 repurchase obligation", "The denominator increases by 2,500 shares under the reverse treasury stock method, reducing diluted EPS to $2.96"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options that are in the money (exercise price exceeds market price) are dilutive. The reverse treasury stock method assumes the entity repurchases 10,000 shares at $50 ($500,000), then reissues shares at the $40 market price (12,500 shares), netting 2,500 incremental shares that increase the denominator. Choice A is incorrect because in-the-money written puts are dilutive, not antidilutive.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Mandatorily convertible instruments in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pendleton Corp. has $500,000 of mandatorily convertible preferred stock that will automatically convert into 20,000 common shares on December 31, 2026. The preferred stock pays a 6% annual dividend. In computing 2025 diluted EPS, the controller excludes these shares because conversion has not yet occurred. What is the most likely effect of this treatment on diluted EPS?', '["Diluted EPS is correctly stated because the conversion has not yet taken place", "Diluted EPS is overstated because mandatorily convertible instruments must be included in diluted EPS from the issuance date", "Diluted EPS is understated because the preferred dividends should still be deducted from the numerator", "Diluted EPS is unaffected because mandatory conversions only impact basic EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-14, mandatorily convertible instruments are included in diluted EPS from the issuance date because conversion is certain. The numerator adds back the $30,000 preferred dividend ($500,000 x 6%) and the denominator adds 20,000 shares. Excluding them overstates diluted EPS by failing to reflect guaranteed dilution. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 does not wait for actual conversion; mandatory conversion requires inclusion in diluted EPS immediately.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Shares issued in business combination weighted from acquisition date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On August 1, Crestline Corp. issues 40,000 common shares to acquire all of the net assets of Dunmore LLC. Before the acquisition, Crestline had 160,000 shares outstanding all year. What is Crestline''s weighted-average number of shares for the year?', '["176,667", "200,000", "180,000", "160,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued in a business combination are included in the weighted-average from the acquisition date. The original 160,000 shares are weighted for the full year, and the 40,000 new shares are weighted from August 1 (5/12 of the year): 160,000 + (40,000 x 5/12) = 176,667. Choice B (200,000) incorrectly includes all shares for the full year without weighting the acquisition shares from the issuance date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Where EPS must be presented
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, where must earnings per share data be reported?', '["Only in the notes to the financial statements", "On the statement of stockholders'' equity", "On the face of the income statement", "In the management discussion and analysis section only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, EPS must be presented on the face of the income statement for all periods for which an income statement is presented. This applies to both basic and diluted EPS. Choice A is incorrect because while supplemental EPS disclosures may appear in the notes, the primary EPS data must be displayed on the income statement itself, not solely in the notes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Multiple dilutive securities with partial-year loss evaluation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Avalon Group reports income from continuing operations of $180,000 in the first half and a loss from continuing operations of $90,000 in the second half, producing full-year income from continuing operations of $90,000. A $40,000 discontinued operations gain brings net income to $130,000. Avalon has 100,000 shares, 5,000 stock options (1,500 incremental shares), and $200,000 of 5% convertible bonds (8,000 conversion shares, tax rate 25%). Evaluate whether each security should be included in diluted EPS.', '["Both securities are antidilutive because of the second-half loss from continuing operations", "Only the stock options are included; the convertible bonds are antidilutive at $0.94 per incremental share versus $0.90 basic EPS", "Both securities are included, but the discontinued operations gain must be excluded from the numerator for testing purposes", "Both securities are included because full-year continuing operations income is positive and both pass the dilution test"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, the control number for dilution testing is full-year income from continuing operations ($90,000), which is positive. Options add zero to the numerator and 1,500 shares, reducing EPS (dilutive). The bonds add $7,500 after-tax interest for 8,000 shares ($0.94 per share), but cumulative diluted EPS still falls below basic, so both are dilutive. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 uses the full annual period, not subperiods, for dilution determination.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Unvested options assumed proceeds include unamortized cost
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Brookfield Industries granted 15,000 unvested stock options with an exercise price of $20 on January 1, 2025. The average unamortized compensation cost is $100,000 and the average market price is $35. Under the treasury stock method, assumed proceeds include the exercise price and unamortized compensation. How many incremental shares are included in diluted EPS?', '["15,000", "3,571", "6,429", "11,429"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28A, assumed proceeds for unvested stock options include the exercise price ($300,000 = 15,000 x $20) plus unamortized compensation cost ($100,000), totaling $400,000. This repurchases 11,429 shares at the $35 market price. The incremental shares are 15,000 minus 11,429, equaling 3,571. Choice C (6,429) incorrectly uses only the exercise price as proceeds, omitting the required unamortized compensation cost adjustment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Two-class method vs. if-converted method evaluation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Kensington Holdings has two classes of preferred stock: Series A is convertible preferred (each share converts to 2 common shares, pays 5% cumulative dividends) and Series B is participating preferred (receives pro-rata dividends alongside common after a 6% preference). The CFO proposes using the if-converted method for both series when computing basic EPS. Evaluate whether this approach is appropriate.', '["The approach is flawed because Series B participating preferred requires the two-class method for basic EPS, not the if-converted method", "The approach is correct because both series are preferred stock and should be treated identically", "The approach is flawed because neither series should affect the basic EPS computation", "The approach is correct if the convertible preferred dividends exceed the participating preferred dividends"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, participating securities that share in undistributed earnings require the two-class method for basic EPS, allocating income between common and participating shares. The if-converted method applies to convertible securities for diluted EPS (ASC 260-10-45-16), not to participating securities for basic EPS. Choice B incorrectly treats both securities identically despite their fundamentally different dividend rights and required computational methods.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Basic EPS formula components
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What are the three components of the basic earnings per share formula under ASC 260?', '["Net income, total equity, and authorized shares", "Operating income, common dividends, and shares outstanding at year-end", "Gross profit, retained earnings, and treasury shares", "Net income, preferred dividends, and weighted-average common shares outstanding"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, basic EPS equals net income minus preferred dividends, divided by weighted-average common shares outstanding during the period. The preferred dividend deduction ensures only income available to common shareholders is reflected. Choice B is incorrect because operating income is not the starting point and year-end shares must be time-weighted rather than used at their point-in-time value.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Diluted EPS with convertible preferred if-converted calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Summerlin Corp. reports net income of $720,000 with 180,000 weighted-average common shares and 10,000 shares of $50 par, 8% convertible preferred stock. Each preferred share converts into 3 common shares. Basic EPS is $3.78 after deducting $40,000 in preferred dividends. What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.78", "$3.60", "$3.43", "$4.00"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-16, the if-converted method adds back the $40,000 preferred dividends to the numerator ($720,000) and adds 30,000 shares (10,000 x 3) to the denominator (210,000 total). Diluted EPS equals $720,000 / 210,000 = $3.43, which is less than basic EPS of $3.78, confirming the conversion is dilutive. Choice A ($3.78) incorrectly presents basic EPS as diluted, failing to apply the if-converted adjustment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Contracts settleable in shares or cash
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgecrest Technologies has a forward contract to sell 15,000 of its own shares for $600,000 in six months. The contract permits Ridgecrest to settle in either cash or shares at its election. The current share price is $45, and the average market price is $42. The CFO argues the contract should be excluded from diluted EPS because management intends to settle in cash. Evaluate whether this treatment is consistent with ASC 260.', '["The treatment is correct because management intent to settle in cash eliminates dilutive potential", "The treatment is incorrect because ASC 260 presumes share settlement for contracts settleable in shares or cash, regardless of management intent", "The treatment is correct only if the cash settlement is more dilutive than share settlement", "The treatment is incorrect because forward contracts are always excluded from diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-45, contracts that give the issuer a choice of settlement in shares or cash must be reflected in diluted EPS using the more dilutive of the two settlement methods. The presumption is share settlement unless cash settlement is more dilutive. Management''s stated intent to settle in cash does not override this requirement. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 does not permit management intent alone to exclude potentially dilutive instruments from the calculation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Contingent shares EPS target not met plus dilutive bonds evaluation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Bridgewater Corp. reports net income of $900,000 with 300,000 weighted-average common shares. It has: (1) 20,000 contingently issuable shares that vest when EPS exceeds $3.50 (current basic EPS is $3.00), and (2) $400,000 of 6% convertible bonds convertible into 15,000 shares (tax rate 25%). The CFO includes the contingent shares in diluted EPS because reaching the $3.50 target is probable, and excludes the bonds as antidilutive. Evaluate whether these decisions are correct.', '["The contingent shares should be excluded because the EPS condition is not currently met, and the convertible bonds should be included because they are dilutive at $1.20 per incremental share", "Both securities should be included in diluted EPS", "The contingent shares should be included and the bonds excluded, as the CFO determined", "Both securities should be excluded from diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-51, contingently issuable shares based on an EPS target are included in diluted EPS only if the condition would be satisfied based on current-period results. Since current basic EPS of $3.00 falls below the $3.50 threshold, the contingent shares are excluded. The convertible bonds produce a per-share impact of $1.20, which is below basic EPS of $3.00, confirming dilution. Choice C reverses both conclusions — probability is irrelevant for contingent shares; only current-period satisfaction matters.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
